In an ASP.NET Core Web API, it's a good practice to keep controllers lightweight and delegate business logic to services. Here’s the recommended sequence of tasks when handling a request in a Controller-Service-Repository pattern:


---

1. Controller (API Layer)

Validate input (Model Binding, Model Validation).

Call the appropriate service method.

Return an appropriate HTTP response.


[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class UserMigrationController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IUserMigrationService _migrationService;

    public UserMigrationController(IUserMigrationService migrationService)
    {
        _migrationService = migrationService;
    }

    [HttpPost("migrate")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> MigrateUsers()
    {
        var result = await _migrationService.MigrateUsersAsync();
        return result ? Ok("Migration completed successfully.") : StatusCode(500, "Migration failed.");
    }
}


---

2. Service (Business Logic Layer)

Fetch data from the database (via repository).

Process and validate data.

Call external APIs (e.g., Azure AD B2C).

Apply business rules.

Return the processed result.


public class UserMigrationService : IUserMigrationService
{
    private readonly IUserRepository _userRepository;
    private readonly IAzureB2CMigrationService _azureB2CMigrationService;

    public UserMigrationService(IUserRepository userRepository, IAzureB2CMigrationService azureB2CMigrationService)
    {
        _userRepository = userRepository;
        _azureB2CMigrationService = azureB2CMigrationService;
    }

    public async Task<bool> MigrateUsersAsync()
    {
        int batchSize = 100;
        var users = await _userRepository.GetUsersForMigrationAsync(batchSize);
        
        if (!users.Any()) return false;

        foreach (var user in users)
        {
            bool isMigrated = await _azureB2CMigrationService.MigrateUserToAzureB2CAsync(user);
            if (isMigrated)
            {
                await _userRepository.MarkUserAsMigratedAsync(user.Id);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}


---

3. Repository (Data Access Layer)

Fetch data from SQL Server using Dapper.

Update the migration status.

Keep database logic separate from business logic.


public class UserRepository : IUserRepository
{
    private readonly IDbConnection _dbConnection;

    public UserRepository(IDbConnection dbConnection)
    {
        _dbConnection = dbConnection;
    }

    public async Task<IEnumerable<User>> GetUsersForMigrationAsync(int batchSize)
    {
        string sql = "SELECT TOP (@BatchSize) * FROM Users WHERE IsMigrated = 0";
        return await _dbConnection.QueryAsync<User>(sql, new { BatchSize = batchSize });
    }

    public async Task MarkUserAsMigratedAsync(int userId)
    {
        string sql = "UPDATE Users SET IsMigrated = 1 WHERE Id = @UserId";
        await _dbConnection.ExecuteAsync(sql, new { UserId = userId });
    }
}


---

Sequence of Execution

1. Client calls API (POST /api/UserMigration/migrate).


2. Controller (UserMigrationController) validates the request and calls the Service (UserMigrationService).


3. Service fetches users from the Repository (UserRepository).


4. Service iterates through users and calls Azure B2C API (IAzureB2CMigrationService).


5. If successful, the Repository marks users as migrated.


6. Service returns success/failure to the Controller.


7. Controller returns an HTTP response.




---

This approach ensures separation of concerns and makes the code maintainable, testable, and scalable. Do you need any modifications based on your requirements?

